<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Puro Mangkunegaran</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1757</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Mangkunegaran Palace</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Puro Mangkunegaran is the palace of the Mangkunegara princes in Surakarta (Solo), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-java">Central Java</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>, begun after the house was founded in 1757. Built in Javanese style around one of the largest open pavilions (pendopo) in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>, it houses collections of gamelan, dance costumes and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>. It remains the residence of the Mangkunegara and a centre of Javanese court culture.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/puro-mangkunegaran.jpg" alt="Puro Mangkunegaran" width="320" height="341" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: RaFaDa20631 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Emblem_of_Mangkunagaran.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
